                "input": "You are an agent in an environment for qualitative inorganic analysis. Your task is to separate each of the cations from a solution containing Hg2+2, Hg+2, Fe+2, and Fe+3 step by step.\nWhich of the following sequences of actions is best suitable for this task?",
                "target_scores": {    
                    "1. Add a solution of H2S in HCl to precipitate mercury as sulfides and filter the precipitate. 2. Dissolving the precipitate from step 1 in nitric acid separates Hg(I) and Hg(II). 3. Add ammonia buffer to the filtrate of step 1 to bring the pH to around 9; Fe(III) precipitates as Fe(OH)3 while Fe(II) remains in the solution.": 0,
                    "1. Add 6M HCl to precipitate Hg(I), and filter the precipitated Hg2Cl2. 2. Bubble H2S through the filtrate of step 1 to precipitate Hg(II) as the sulfide and filter the precipitate. 3. Add ammonia buffer to the filtrate of step 2 to bring up the pH to around 8; Fe(III) precipitates as Fe(OH)3 while Fe(II) remains in the solution.": 1,
                    "1. Add a solution of KI to the sample and filter the HgI2 that precipitates. 2. Add 6M HCl to the filtrate from step 1 to precipitate Hg(I) and filter the Hg2Cl2 that precipitates. 3. Add a buffered (NH4)2S solution (pH around 9) to the filtrate from step 2; Fe(II) precipitates as FeS while Fe+3 remains in solution.": 0,
                    "1. Add a NH4Cl/NH3 buffer (pH around 8) to precipitate Fe+3 as Fe(OH)3 and filter it. 2. Bubble H2S through the solution to precipitate Hg(II) and Fe(II) as sulfides while Hg(I) remains in solution. 3. Filter the precipitate from step 2 and redissolve in nitric acid. FeS dissolves but HgS remains as a solid.": 0
